WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:16.440
Around the world,

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.830
businesses field hundreds of billions

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:20.393
of requests from us each year.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:23.490
We want our flights changed,

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:26.103
a purchase refunded, a claim reviewed.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.840
That takes the labor of millions of agents

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.443
who tend to our every need.

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:36.313
But soon, automation will
take over much of that,

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:41.383
and it starts with the painstaking
work of this 23-year-old.

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:45.313
My name is Laura Morales,
and I'm a chatbot designer.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:09.133
You probably know

00:01:09.133 --> 00:01:11.553
the Dominican Republic for its beaches.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.963
But further west in the country's
capital of Santo Domingo,

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:25.260
there's a bustling hub of call centers

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:26.853
for American businesses.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:33.593
Laura works for one of them,
at a company called OutPLEX.

00:01:35.413 --> 00:01:36.246
After you.

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:39.200
Some of OutPLEX's inquiries

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.510
are handled by traditional
call center agents

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:43.270
over the phone.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:45.810
Others are handled by
contact center agents

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:47.193
over live written chat.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:52.150
Last year, the company
introduced a third kind of agent.

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:52.983
A bot.

00:01:56.110 --> 00:02:00.090
Laura oversees bots for
three U.S.-based clients.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:02.080
And these bots greet customers

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:03.883
looking for assistance online.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:08.182
The bots resolve simple
queries on their own,

00:02:08.182 --> 00:02:09.800
and the more complicated questions

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.223
get escalated to a human representative.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:16.530
Me, as an agent, I used to
take three conversations,

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:19.120
or just one call at the time.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:22.093
With bots, you don't have that limitation.

00:02:26.247 --> 00:02:27.700
Alexa, will I need

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:29.800
an umbrella tomorrow?

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:31.370
It might rain tomorrow.

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:33.453
There's a 54% chance.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.950
Bots today are everywhere.

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:38.930
And so the people who design their speech

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:40.113
are in high demand.

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:44.700
The job is kinda like writing
a very dry screenplay,

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.550
with a choose your own adventure element

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:48.893
for the many ways customers will respond.

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:53.366
The hard part comes
once the bot goes live.

00:02:53.366 --> 00:02:55.990
Can you go to the metrics hub over there?

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:58.190
Often, its pre-scripted conversations

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:01.710
don't work out the way Laura
and her team hope they would,

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:04.010
so they're constantly tinkering.

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:07.160
The way to start a day with the bots.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:10.980
I need to check how the bots
did on the previous day.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.830
So, I go into analytics,

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:16.000
and my favorite one is this.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.070
It's the unmatched phrases.

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:20.120
These are the moments
when the bot didn't know

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:21.720
what the customer wanted.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.230
Exactly.

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:26.410
And I can also see how
many clients or visitors

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:28.940
clicked on the first
button or the second button

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:30.150
on the main menu.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:33.510
So, if my visitors are not
interacting with my menus,

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:36.790
that means that maybe
something's not right.

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:39.966
So, it's interesting 'cause
you used to be coaching

00:03:39.966 --> 00:03:44.500
the human agents, and now
you're coaching a robot.

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:45.913
Is that easier or harder?

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:48.970
It's easier.

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:51.610
You don't have to worry about
hurting a bot's feelings.

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:53.870
You're not emotional about it.

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:56.370
You're just doing what you need to do

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:58.020
for getting the results you want.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:10.488
Laura grew up hearing
all about the industry.

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:50.530
Laura started out as an entry-level agent

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:53.363
when she was 17, and worked her way up.

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:57.690
Last year, she was chosen
to manage her company's

00:04:57.690 --> 00:04:59.990
first interactive chatbot.

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:02.670
And today makes about $8 an hour,

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:04.480
which is four times what she earned

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.183
when she first became an agent.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:13.580
The transition to work on chatbots

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:17.380
took about three months to learn
various aspects of the job,

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:19.570
including training on special software

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:21.283
that doesn't require her to code.

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:25.003
Laura immediately saw the benefits.

00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:29.030
A bot is never late.

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:32.193
A bot doesn't get sick or pregnant.

00:05:33.850 --> 00:05:38.383
Those are specific human
situations that you can't fight.

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:42.253
Automation is able to take that out.

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:46.470
But her mom had some hesitations.

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:00.350
For now, the introduction of the bots

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:02.820
hasn't led to any layoffs.

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:05.880
Because they've helped
OutPLEX win more business,

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:09.120
the company has actually
hired more human agents

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:12.233
to handle the inquiries that
get escalated from the bots.

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:21.930
But as the technology gets better,

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:24.930
it's hard not to worry about
how this is all gonna play out.

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:28.140
And that was a conversation

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:30.493
that required some strong Dominican rum.

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:39.970
Eventually, surely we will need

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:44.970
fewer human call center
agents than we do now.

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:47.460
Automation is a reality.

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:50.450
And the skill set that you will need

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:51.980
to work in this industry,

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:54.950
it's going to be different
from the regular agents

00:06:54.950 --> 00:06:56.530
you have today.

00:06:56.530 --> 00:06:59.290
I do believe it will get more technical.

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:02.100
It will not be dealing
with a customer anymore.

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:05.580
It will be monitoring the software.

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:08.993
And if you don't adapt, you
will need to do something else.

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:14.060
Is there a small part
of you that feels guilty

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:16.860
for automating away the job

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:20.670
that gave you your start in your career

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:24.363
and is the job of a lot of
your colleagues right now.

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:27.350
Not at all.

00:07:27.350 --> 00:07:29.750
Like, zero guilt.

00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:30.583
Zero guilt?

00:07:30.583 --> 00:07:31.416
Yeah.

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:34.123
Zero hesitation?

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:38.180
It's happening already.

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:40.370
You might as well be a part of it.

00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:41.720
Thank God I'm part of it.

00:07:53.952 --> 00:07:56.100
Whether you're a grocer, doctor,

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:58.073
factory worker, or journalist,

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:03.453
all of our jobs will soon
be reshaped by automation.

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:10.113
Some will benefit from the
new work that will emerge,

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:15.320
and others will watch their jobs disappear

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:17.483
with no clear path to another livelihood.

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:23.190
Managing this transition will
be the defining challenge

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:24.803
for us in the decades ahead.

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:28.270
And we need to be ready for it.

